The only kinship statement in these verses is in 9.4.71, where Nābhāga's son (the king) is named by the phrase "nābhāgatanayaṃ nṛpam". The verse does not name the king himself; the other verses name no relations. The king's name is not given in these verses, so the statement cannot be written with a named "a". Per the rules ("no persons the verses do not name"), I do not record it.

Other verses contain no genealogical relations: "dārāgāraputrāpta" in 9.4.65 is generic, and "satstriyaḥ satpatiṃ" in 9.4.66 is a simile.

